    "q-79813": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79813",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "If $f(x,y)=xy+x{e}^{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y+{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}x+(x+x{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,0) & =(0+{e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}x+(2+2\\cdot {e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+4\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-2=-1, \\\\ Δy & =-0.5-0=-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & ≈-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
