    "q-167171": {
      "dom_id": "step-q167171",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Estimate $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)$ at the point $({\\pi }^{2},0).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $({\\pi }^{2},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x-\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,Δx-\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)=f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $({\\pi }^{2},0)$ to $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)-{\\pi }^{2}=1, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈(-\\frac{1}{2\\pi })\\cdot (1)-1\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }-0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $-0.359.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2) & ≈f({\\pi }^{2},0)-0.359 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2) & ≈\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}+0)-0.359 \\\\  & =0-0.359 \\\\  & =-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
